@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
' purpose: EXPERIMENT — can ONE browser engine serve every def over CDP, instead of a `-browser` image per def?
' status: proposed 2026-09-10, not implemented; nothing in src depends on this file
' would touch: internal/sbx/run.go (CDPBrowserPort, CDPRelayPort, cdpRelay, BrowserCDPArgs)
' would touch: internal/backend/sandbox/sandbox.go (cdpPublish, StartCDPViewport, AGENT_BROWSER_ARGS)
' would touch: defs/base-node-browser/Dockerfile, internal/contract/browser_layer_test.go
'   the question this answers ...... _spec/_devops/all-defs-green.puml (R4)
'   what the layer carries today ... _spec/defs/browser-layer.puml
'   why the host cannot reach in ... _spec/internal/sbx/browser-viewport.puml
'   the reach this needs, unbuilt .. _spec/_plans/tools-and-localhost-tunnels.puml
'   the prize .................... .. _spec/_plans/image-size-reduction.puml
'   the operator-Chrome case, gated  _spec/_plans/claude-in-chrome-reachability.puml
' Notes are capped at 5 lines x 50 columns; one idea per anchor.
'
' ── AUTHORITATIVE SOURCES ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
'   agent-browser : https://github.com/vercel-labs/agent-browser
'   sbx ports     : https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/sbx/ports/
'
' ── THE QUESTION ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
' R4 in the requirement sheet asks whether `cecli-browser` is absent by design.
' It is not — but the reason it looked absent by design points at something
' larger: if agent-browser can drive an engine it did not launch, then the
' expensive half of the browser layer need not be in any def's image.
'
' So: can ONE engine serve every def, and what does that cost?
'
' ── THE LEVER EXISTS, AND IT TAKES A REMOTE URL ────────────────────────────
' agent-browser documents `--cdp <port|url>`, or `AGENT_BROWSER_CDP`: "a port
' number (e.g., 9222) for local connections ... or a full WebSocket URL (e.g.,
' wss://... or ws://...) for remote browser services." There is also
' `--auto-connect` / `AGENT_BROWSER_AUTO_CONNECT` for discovery.
'
' A remote engine is therefore a SUPPORTED MODE rather than a trick, and proveo
' already has the wiring point: the browser layer sets AGENT_BROWSER_* variables
' (EXECUTABLE_PATH, SKILLS_DIR) and sandbox.Spec injects AGENT_BROWSER_ARGS. One
' more variable is the entire client-side change.
'
' ── WHAT EXISTS TODAY POINTS THE OTHER WAY ─────────────────────────────────
' The CDP plumbing in this tree runs INWARD-OUT, for a human:
'   CDPBrowserPort = 9223   Chromium's own DevTools port, LOOPBACK ONLY — which
'                           is why a relay is needed at all
'   cdpRelay                a Python TCP forwarder run via `sbx exec`, binding
'                           0.0.0.0:CDPRelayPort and piping to 127.0.0.1:9223
'   cdpPublish              publishes that relay port to the host
'   StartCDPViewport        holds the host end while the run lives
' Browser inside, operator watching from outside (browser-viewport.puml).
'
' THE PROBE INVERTS THAT. Engine outside, agent inside, connecting out — which
' is the SANDBOX -> HOST direction, and that direction is the unbuilt half of
' tools-and-localhost-tunnels.puml. This experiment depends on that plan; it
' cannot be measured before it.
'
' ── THE FINDING THAT DECIDES THE TOPOLOGY, BEFORE ANY MEASUREMENT ──────────
' There are two very different "outside"s, and only one of them is this.
'
'   A SIDECAR ENGINE, inside the policy. Its own traffic still leaves through
'     proveo's egress path, so the allowlist still governs what the agent can
'     reach by browsing. This is the shape that collapses the image variants.
'   THE OPERATOR'S OWN CHROME, on the host. Its traffic leaves through the
'     HOST'S network, not the sandbox's. So an agent driving it browses AROUND
'     the egress boundary entirely — every allow rule, every tier, every policy
'     log becomes decorative for anything fetched through that browser.
'
' A host-side engine is therefore not a smaller version of this idea; it is an
' EXFILTRATION PATH wearing the same API. That case already exists, is already
' gated, and is already specced as its own thing — claude-in-chrome, with
' `chromebridge.ScopeGate` — and it must not be reached by accident through a
' variable meant for image size.
'
' SO THE TOPOLOGY IS DECIDED IN ADVANCE: sidecar, never host. That is a
' constraint on the experiment, not an outcome of it.
'
' ── AND THE CDP ROUTE COSTS agent-browser ITS OWN CONTAINMENT ──────────────
' Quoted, because it is the kind of thing a probe discovers too late: with
' `--cdp` or `--auto-connect`, "pre-existing CDP sessions, auto-connect, Chrome
' profiles, direct-page provider plugins, agent-browser restore or state-file
' replay ... reject this option because agent-browser cannot install equivalent
' containment before page scripts run" — the option being its domain allowlist.
'
' So connecting to an engine it did not launch means agent-browser's OWN
' per-domain containment is unavailable. On a tree whose posture is egress
' allowlisting that is survivable — proveo's control is at the proxy, not in the
' tool — but it is a real reduction, and it is only survivable in the sidecar
' topology above. In the host topology it removes the second fence after the
' first one was already bypassed.
'
' ── CDP IS NOT AN AUTH BOUNDARY, WHICH MATTERS ONCE IT IS SHARED ──────────
' A CDP endpoint is total control of the browser: any page, any cookie, any
' stored session. One engine serving several runs therefore needs REAL isolation
' per run, not politeness.
'
' agent-browser claims it: "each session has its own: browser instance, cookies
' and storage, navigation history, authentication state". That is the right
' claim and it is the one to verify at the CDP level rather than trust — because
' the failure is silent and cross-run, and this tree already has a case of one
' run's state informing another (the stale `sbx secret`).
'
' ── THE CHEAPER THING TO TRY FIRST ────────────────────────────────────────
' If the goal is image size, a shared engine is not the only route. agent-browser
' supports `--engine lightpanda` beside the default `chrome`. A lightweight
' engine IN the image may well be smaller than Chromium plus a relay plus a
' sidecar plus an inbound tunnel — and it keeps every def self-contained, which
' is worth a great deal on its own.
'
' Measure that before building topology. An experiment that skips the cheap
' alternative and builds the interesting one is how this tree has previously
' arrived at elaborate answers to questions that had simple ones.
'
' ── EXPERIMENTS — NOTHING BELOW IS MEASURED ─────────────────────────────────
' H1  DOES `--engine lightpanda` DO THE JOB, IN-IMAGE?
'     Cheapest, and it may end the experiment. Measure the layer size against
'     Chromium's, then run the existing browser suites against it. If it passes,
'     no tunnel, no sidecar, no shared state, and every def stays
'     self-contained.
'
' H2  DOES agent-browser DRIVE A REMOTE ENGINE AT ALL, HERE?
'     `AGENT_BROWSER_CDP=ws://...` against a Chromium in a sidecar, from inside
'     a run. The documented mode, unverified on this stack. Note that
'     CDPBrowserPort is loopback-only by design, so the sidecar needs the same
'     relay treatment the in-sandbox browser already gets — the existing
'     `cdpRelay` may be reusable verbatim in the other direction.
'
' H3  IS PER-RUN ISOLATION REAL AT THE CDP LEVEL?
'     Two runs against one engine: set a cookie and a logged-in session in one,
'     and try to read them from the other. A pass here is the difference between
'     a shared engine and a shared identity. Adversarial, not polite.
'
' H4  WHAT DOES THE LOST DOMAIN CONTAINMENT ACTUALLY PERMIT?
'     With `--cdp` in force, fetch a host the run's allowlist forbids and see
'     which fence stops it. The answer should be "proveo's proxy" — and if it is
'     not, the sidecar topology is not as contained as this file assumes.
'
' H5  DOES THE PRIZE SURVIVE THE MACHINERY?
'     Total the cost — a sidecar image, a relay, an allow entry, a published
'     port, per-run isolation — against four `-browser` variants deleted. If the
'     sidecar plus plumbing is not decisively smaller, the honest answer is to
'     leave the variants alone and give cecli one.
'
' ── WHAT THIS WOULD BUY, AND WHY IT IS WORTH THE PROBE ────────────────────
' Four `-browser` variants exist — claudecode, cursor, opencode, and the cecli
' one that does not. Each carries a Chromium. If one engine can serve them all,
' image-size-reduction.puml gets its largest single win and R4's gap closes as a
' side effect rather than as a rebase.
'
' If it cannot, the fallback is small and already understood: rebase cecli onto
' `base-node-browser` and build `cecli-browser` like the other three. That is
' the outcome to accept quickly if H1 or H2 says so, rather than the one to
' avoid.
title EXPERIMENT — one engine over CDP, or a lighter engine per image;\nand why the engine may never live on the host

package "the sandbox" as SB <<host>> {
  component "the agent\nMCP client, stdio" as AG <<app>>
  component "agent-browser\n""AGENT_BROWSER_CDP=ws://…""" as AB <<app>>
}
component "**sidecar engine**\ninside the egress policy" as SIDE <<app>>
component "**the operator's Chrome**\noutside it" as HOSTCH <<error>>
cloud "proveo's egress path\nallowlist · policy log" as EGRESS <<cloud>>
component "the internet" as NET <<cloud>>
component "**a second run**\nsharing one engine" as RUN2 <<error>>
component """--engine lightpanda""\nin-image, no topology" as LP <<app>>

AG ARROW_MAIN AB : spawns it — stdio MCP,\nso the binary is local
AB ARROW_MAIN SIDE : the documented ""--cdp"" mode
SIDE ARROW_MAIN EGRESS
EGRESS ARROW_CLOUD NET : the allowlist still governs
AB ARROW_ERROR HOSTCH : the same variable, the\nwrong outside
HOSTCH ARROW_ERROR NET : leaves by the HOST's network —\naround every allow rule
RUN2 ARROW_ERROR SIDE : cookies · sessions · storage,\nunless isolation is real
LP ARROW_OPTIONAL AB : the cheap alternative,\nmeasured first

note bottom of HOSTCH
  **Not a smaller version of this idea.** A
  host-side engine's traffic leaves by the host's
  network, so browsing it bypasses the allowlist,
  the tier and the policy log. That case is
  claude-in-chrome, and it is gated for a reason.
end note

note bottom of SIDE
  **Inside the policy is the whole point.** A
  sidecar's own traffic still leaves through
  proveo's egress path, so the allowlist keeps
  governing what the agent can reach by
  browsing. Sidecar, never host.
end note

note bottom of RUN2
  **CDP is total control, not an auth boundary.**
  Any page, any cookie, any session. Shared
  across runs it needs REAL per-run isolation —
  agent-browser claims it, and H3 verifies it
  adversarially rather than trusting the claim.
end note

note bottom of LP
  **Try the cheap thing first.** A lightweight
  engine in-image may beat Chromium plus a relay
  plus a sidecar plus a tunnel — and it keeps
  every def self-contained, which is worth a lot
  by itself.
end note

note right of AB
  **The client must be local either way.** The MCP
  server is stdio, so the agent spawns the
  binary. Only the ENGINE moves; the driver stays
  in the image, which is why the split is worth
  anything at all.
end note

legend bottom
  **What this file settles before measuring, and what it measures**
  ==
  **Settled in advance: sidecar, never host.** A host-side engine's traffic leaves by the
  host's network, so an agent driving it browses AROUND the allowlist, the tier and the
  policy log. That is an exfiltration path wearing the same API as an image-size
  optimisation, and it must not be reached by accident through a variable meant for one.
  --
  **Also settled: the CDP mode costs agent-browser its own containment.** Its docs say so
  outright — with ""--cdp"" it "cannot install equivalent containment before page scripts
  run". Survivable when proveo's proxy is the fence; not survivable when it is not.
  ==
  **H1 first, because it may end the experiment**: ""--engine lightpanda"" in-image needs no
  tunnel, no sidecar and no shared state. Only if that fails is the topology worth
  building — and H5 asks whether the machinery is smaller than the four variants it deletes.
  --
  **The fallback is fine**: rebase cecli onto ""base-node-browser"" and build ""cecli-browser""
  like the other three. That is the answer to accept quickly, not the one to avoid.
end legend
@enduml
